lessons.push(
{id:'measurement-vectors',title:'Measurement, scalars & vectors',topic:'01 · Measurement skills',minutes:25,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Choose suitable instruments, improve measurements and combine vectors.',
objectives:['Select instruments and read scales correctly.','Use repeated measurements and calculate a mean.','Distinguish scalars from vectors and find right-angle resultants.'],
sections:[
section('Choose a suitable instrument','Use a ruler for ordinary lengths, vernier calipers for internal or external diameters, and a micrometer for very small thicknesses. A measuring cylinder measures liquid volume; displacement gives the volume of an irregular solid that sinks. Use a balance for mass and a stopwatch or electronic timing system for time.','Match the instrument range and resolution to the size of the quantity.'),
section('Read a scale reliably','Check the zero before measuring. View a scale perpendicular to it to reduce parallax. Record the value with the unit and precision justified by the scale divisions. For a liquid in a suitable measuring cylinder, read the correct part of the meniscus at eye level.','A measurement without a unit is incomplete.'),
section('Reduce random uncertainty','Measure a longer combined interval or dimension, then divide. For example, time 20 oscillations to find one period or measure the thickness of a stack of sheets. Repeat independent readings and calculate a mean, checking any anomalous result before deciding whether to repeat it.','Repeats reduce random variation but do not correct a zero error.'),
section('Scalars and vectors','Scalars have magnitude only: distance, speed, mass, time, energy and temperature. Vectors have magnitude and direction: displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum and field strength. Add collinear vectors using signs. For perpendicular vectors, draw a scale diagram or use Pythagoras for the magnitude.','State a direction with a vector result.')],
formulas:[['Mean','sum of readings / number of readings','same as reading'],['Right-angle resultant','R = √(x² + y²)','vector unit'],['Period','T = total time / number of oscillations','s']],
examples:[
example('Measure one sheet','A stack of 80 paper sheets is 7.2 mm thick. Estimate one-sheet thickness.',['Divide the combined thickness by the number of sheets.','Thickness = 7.2/80 = 0.090 mm.','The stack makes the total easier to measure accurately.'],'0.090 mm','The same reading uncertainty is a smaller fraction of a larger measurement.'),
example('Perpendicular velocity','A boat moves east at 6.0 m/s while the river carries it north at 8.0 m/s. Find resultant speed.',['The components are perpendicular.','v = √(6.0² + 8.0²).','v = √100 = 10 m/s.'],'10 m/s','Direction could also be found from a scale diagram or trigonometry when required.')],
questions:[mc('Which instrument is most suitable for wire diameter?',['Metre rule','Micrometer','Measuring cylinder'],1,'A micrometer resolves very small diameters.'),mc('Which is a scalar?',['Momentum','Velocity','Energy'],2,'Energy has magnitude but no direction.'),mc('Why time many pendulum oscillations?',['To increase gravity','To reduce fractional timing uncertainty','To remove every systematic error'],1,'A longer interval reduces the relative effect of reaction time.'),num('Ten periods take 16.0 s. Find one period.',1.6,'s','T = 16.0/10 = 1.60 s.'),num('Perpendicular forces are 5 N and 12 N. Find resultant magnitude.',13,'N','R = √(5² + 12²) = 13 N.'),explain('Explain the difference between random and systematic error.',['Random error makes repeated readings scatter and can be reduced by repeats and averaging.','Systematic error shifts readings in a consistent way and requires correcting the method or calibration.'])]},
{id:'density-pressure',title:'Density, pressure & fluids',topic:'01 · Matter and pressure',minutes:25,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Relate mass to volume, predict floating and calculate pressure in solids and fluids.',
objectives:['Measure and calculate density.','Use pressure as force per area.','Explain pressure changes with depth.'],
sections:[
section('Density','Density is mass per unit volume. Use consistent units such as kg/m³ or g/cm³. Find regular-solid volume from dimensions and irregular-solid volume by displacement. For a liquid, subtract the empty container mass from the filled mass, then divide by liquid volume.','Convert all quantities into one consistent unit system before dividing.'),
section('Floating','An object floats when it can displace enough fluid for upthrust to balance its weight. Given density data, an object less dense than a liquid can float; a denser one sinks if other effects are ignored. Immiscible liquids arrange with the less dense liquid above the denser one.','Floating does not mean weight is zero; upthrust balances it.'),
section('Pressure on a surface','Pressure is normal force per unit area. The same force gives larger pressure on a smaller area, explaining sharp blades and wide snowshoes. One pascal is one newton per square metre. Use the contact area actually supporting the force.','Convert cm² to m² carefully: 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m².'),
section('Pressure in a liquid','Liquid pressure increases with depth and density: Δp = ρgΔh. At the same depth in one connected liquid, pressure is the same. The pressure difference creates forces on submerged surfaces and contributes to upthrust.','Use vertical depth, not the sloping distance along a container.')],
formulas:[['Density','ρ = m/V','kg/m³'],['Pressure','p = F/A','Pa'],['Liquid pressure change','Δp = ρgΔh','Pa']],
examples:[
example('Irregular-solid density','A stone has mass 135 g. Water rises from 50 cm³ to 100 cm³ when it is immersed. Find density.',['Stone volume = 100 − 50 = 50 cm³.','ρ = m/V = 135/50.','ρ = 2.7 g/cm³.'],'2.7 g/cm³','The rise in water volume equals the submerged stone volume.'),
example('Pressure under a block','A 600 N block rests on a face of area 0.030 m². Find pressure.',['Use p = F/A.','p = 600/0.030.','p = 20 000 Pa.'],'2.0 × 10⁴ Pa','The force must be normal to the contact surface.')],
questions:[mc('An object floats in water when its average density is…',['less than water density','always zero','greater than water density'],0,'It can displace water until upthrust balances weight.'),mc('Which change increases pressure for the same force?',['Larger area','Smaller area','Greater volume only'],1,'p = F/A.'),mc('Liquid pressure at greater depth is generally…',['smaller','unchanged','larger'],2,'There is a taller column of liquid above.'),num('Mass is 240 g and volume is 80 cm³. Find density.',3,'g/cm³','ρ = 240/80 = 3 g/cm³.'),num('A 500 N force acts over 0.25 m². Find pressure.',2000,'Pa','p = 500/0.25 = 2000 Pa.'),explain('Describe how to determine the density of an irregular solid that sinks.',['Measure its mass with a balance and volume by liquid displacement.','Calculate density = mass/volume using consistent units.'])]},
{id:'deformation-moments',title:'Springs, moments & equilibrium',topic:'01 · Forces and turning',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Interpret load–extension graphs and apply turning-effect and equilibrium conditions.',
objectives:['Use spring constant and identify proportional behaviour.','Calculate moments.','Apply equilibrium and centre-of-gravity ideas.'],
sections:[
section('Load and extension','Extension is stretched length minus original length. In the proportional region, force is proportional to extension and F = kx. The gradient of a force–extension graph is spring constant k. The limit of proportionality is where the straight-line relationship ends.','Do not confuse final length with extension.'),
section('Spring experiment','Measure original length, add known loads, allow oscillations to settle, and record new length. Repeat while loading and keep within a safe range. Plot force against extension. A wide range and a best-fit line help reveal proportional behaviour.','A force–extension graph and an extension–force graph have reciprocal gradients.'),
section('Moments','The moment of a force about a pivot is force × perpendicular distance from pivot to the force line of action. Clockwise and anticlockwise moments balance in rotational equilibrium. A couple is a pair of equal opposite forces producing a turning effect.','Use perpendicular distance, not necessarily the length of the object.'),
section('Equilibrium and centre of gravity','For equilibrium, resultant force is zero and resultant moment is zero. Centre of gravity is the point where the object weight can be considered to act. Stability increases with a lower centre of gravity and wider base because the weight line remains within the base through a larger tilt.','An object topples when the weight line falls outside its base.')],
formulas:[['Extension','x = stretched length − original length','m'],['Spring relationship','F = kx','N'],['Moment','moment = force × perpendicular distance','N m']],
examples:[
example('Spring constant','A 6.0 N force produces an extension of 0.030 m in the proportional region. Find k.',['Use k = F/x.','k = 6.0/0.030.','k = 200 N/m.'],'200 N/m','Use extension in metres for N/m.'),
example('Balancing moments','A 30 N force acts 0.40 m left of a pivot. What force 0.60 m right balances it?',['Anticlockwise moment = 30 × 0.40 = 12 N m.','For balance: F × 0.60 = 12.','F = 20 N.'],'20 N','Balanced moments have equal magnitude and opposite sense.')],
questions:[mc('On a force–extension graph, the proportional region is…',['a straight line through the origin','any horizontal line','after the spring breaks'],0,'F is proportional to x there.'),mc('Moment uses distance measured…',['parallel to the force','perpendicular to the line of action','from either end only'],1,'The lever arm is the perpendicular distance from pivot.'),mc('An object is more stable with…',['a high centre of gravity and narrow base','a low centre of gravity and wide base','zero mass'],1,'Its weight line stays within the base for a greater tilt.'),num('A 10 N force acts 0.25 m from a pivot. Find moment.',2.5,'N m','Moment = 10 × 0.25 = 2.5 N m.'),num('A spring of k = 300 N/m extends by 0.020 m. Find force.',6,'N','F = 300 × 0.020 = 6 N.'),explain('State both conditions required for an object to be in equilibrium.',['The resultant force is zero.','The resultant moment about any point is zero.'])]},
{id:'momentum',title:'Momentum, impulse & collisions',topic:'01 · Momentum',minutes:30,levels:['Extended'],
intro:'Calculate momentum changes and use conservation in one-dimensional interactions.',
objectives:['Use momentum and impulse equations.','Apply conservation of momentum.','Relate force to rate of momentum change.'],
sections:[
section('Momentum','Momentum p = mv is a vector. Choose a positive direction and assign signs to velocities. Its unit is kg m/s. A heavy slow object and a light fast object can have equal momentum.','Direction matters; opposite momenta have opposite signs.'),
section('Conservation','In a closed system with no external resultant force, total momentum before an interaction equals total momentum after. Apply this to collisions, recoil and explosions. Kinetic energy is not necessarily conserved, even when momentum is.','Include every interacting object in the chosen system.'),
section('Impulse','Impulse equals force × time and equals change in momentum. Increasing the time for a given momentum change reduces average force, which explains airbags, crumple zones and bending the knees when landing.','Use final momentum minus initial momentum, with signs.'),
section('Force and momentum rate','Resultant force equals rate of change of momentum, F = Δp/Δt. For constant mass this leads to F = ma, but the momentum form is more general. A force–time graph area gives impulse.','The gradient of a momentum–time graph gives resultant force.')],
formulas:[['Momentum','p = mv','kg m/s'],['Impulse','J = FΔt = Δp','N s'],['Force','F = Δp/Δt','N']],
examples:[
example('Objects stick together','A 2.0 kg trolley at 4.0 m/s hits a stationary 3.0 kg trolley and they stick. Find common velocity.',['Initial momentum = 2.0 × 4.0 = 8.0 kg m/s.','Combined mass = 5.0 kg.','v = 8.0/5.0 = 1.6 m/s in the original direction.'],'1.6 m/s','Momentum is conserved; kinetic energy decreases in this inelastic collision.'),
example('Average force','A 0.15 kg ball moving at 20 m/s is stopped in 0.060 s. Find average force magnitude.',['Initial momentum = 0.15 × 20 = 3.0 kg m/s.','Change magnitude = 3.0 kg m/s.','F = 3.0/0.060 = 50 N.'],'50 N','The force direction is opposite the initial motion.')],
questions:[mc('Momentum is…',['a scalar','a vector','measured in joules'],1,'It includes velocity direction.'),mc('Momentum is conserved when the chosen system has…',['no external resultant force','no kinetic energy','zero mass'],0,'Internal forces occur in equal and opposite pairs.'),mc('An airbag reduces force mainly by…',['increasing stopping time','increasing momentum change','removing mass'],0,'For the same Δp, larger Δt gives smaller average force.'),num('A 5 kg object moves at 3 m/s. Find momentum.',15,'kg m/s','p = 5 × 3 = 15 kg m/s.'),num('Momentum changes by 12 kg m/s in 0.20 s. Find average force.',60,'N','F = 12/0.20 = 60 N.'),explain('Why can momentum be conserved in a collision while kinetic energy decreases?',['Total momentum is conserved in an isolated system.','Some kinetic energy is transferred to internal energy, sound or deformation.'])]},
{id:'energy-resources',title:'Energy resources & efficiency',topic:'01 · Energy systems',minutes:25,levels:['Core','Extended'],
intro:'Trace energy transfers, compare resources and calculate useful efficiency.',
objectives:['Describe energy stores and transfers.','Calculate efficiency.','Evaluate renewable and non-renewable resources.'],
sections:[
section('Stores and transfers','Energy can be stored kinetically, gravitationally, elastically, chemically, thermally, magnetically, electrostatically or nuclearly. Transfers occur mechanically, electrically, by heating or by waves. In every process total energy is conserved, though some becomes less useful as it spreads into the surroundings.','Avoid saying energy is used up; describe where it is transferred.'),
section('Efficiency','Efficiency compares useful output with total input, using either energy or power. It can be written as a fraction or percentage and cannot exceed 100%. Reducing friction, unwanted heating or sound can improve efficiency.','Useful output depends on the intended function of the device.'),
section('Resource comparisons','Fossil fuels are reliable and energy dense but finite and release carbon dioxide; combustion can also release pollutants. Nuclear fission is low-carbon during operation and reliable but creates radioactive waste and has high construction costs. Solar and wind are renewable but intermittent; hydroelectric, tidal, wave, geothermal and biofuel each have location and environmental trade-offs.','Compare reliability, environmental impact, cost and renewability rather than naming one universal best resource.'),
section('Power and demand','Power is the rate of energy transfer. Electricity supply must match changing demand. Storage, a varied generation mix and controllable stations help respond to peaks and intermittent renewable output.','A high-power device transfers energy faster; it does not necessarily use more total energy unless operating time is known.')],
formulas:[['Efficiency','useful output / total input','fraction'],['Percentage efficiency','useful output / total input × 100%','%'],['Energy transferred','E = Pt','J']],
examples:[
example('Device efficiency','A motor receives 500 J and transfers 350 J usefully. Find efficiency.',['Efficiency = useful/input.','Efficiency = 350/500 = 0.70.','Percentage efficiency = 70%.'],'70%','The remaining 150 J is transferred in unwanted ways.'),
example('Electrical energy','A 2.0 kW heater operates for 3.0 minutes. Find energy in joules.',['2.0 kW = 2000 W.','3.0 min = 180 s.','E = Pt = 2000 × 180 = 360 000 J.'],'3.6 × 10⁵ J','Convert power to watts and time to seconds.')],
questions:[mc('Which is a renewable resource?',['Coal','Wind','Natural gas'],1,'Wind is continually replenished.'),mc('Efficiency can be greater than 100%…',['always','only for motors','never'],2,'Useful output cannot exceed total input.'),mc('A major limitation of solar generation is…',['intermittency','radioactive waste','fuel combustion'],0,'Output depends on available sunlight.'),num('Useful output is 240 J from 300 J input. Find percentage efficiency.',80,'%','240/300 × 100 = 80%.'),num('A 50 W device runs for 40 s. Find energy.',2000,'J','E = 50 × 40 = 2000 J.'),explain('Give one advantage and one disadvantage of nuclear power compared with fossil-fuel generation.',['It produces very low operational carbon dioxide emissions and is reliable.','It creates radioactive waste and involves high costs or accident-management requirements.'])]},
{id:'gas-laws-expansion',title:'Gas laws & thermal expansion',topic:'02 · Thermal properties',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Use the kelvin scale, explain gas behaviour and compare expansion in states of matter.',
objectives:['Convert Celsius to kelvin.','Use pV = constant at constant temperature.','Explain thermal expansion and its applications.'],
sections:[
section('Absolute temperature','Convert using T/K = θ/°C + 273. The kelvin scale starts at absolute zero. A temperature interval has the same numerical size in K and °C, but absolute temperatures are not found by simply relabelling Celsius values.','Use kelvin for gas relationships involving absolute temperature.'),
section('Gas pressure','Gas particles move randomly and exert forces when they collide with walls. At constant volume, heating increases average kinetic energy, causing more frequent and forceful collisions, so pressure rises. At constant temperature, reducing volume increases collision frequency and pressure.','Do not explain gas pressure as the weight of particles in ordinary sealed-container questions.'),
section('Boyle relationship','For a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, pV = constant. Pressure is inversely proportional to volume, so a p–V graph is a curve while p against 1/V is a straight line through the origin. Use the same pressure and volume units on both sides.','The relationship requires constant temperature and fixed gas mass.'),
section('Thermal expansion','Heating generally increases average particle separation. Gases expand much more than liquids, and liquids more than solids. Applications and consequences include liquid thermometers, expansion gaps in bridges, bimetallic strips and slack in overhead cables.','Expansion is caused by changed particle separation, not larger particles.')],
formulas:[['Absolute temperature','T = θ + 273','K'],['Constant-temperature gas','p₁V₁ = p₂V₂','consistent units']],
examples:[
example('Celsius to kelvin','Convert 27 °C to kelvin.',['Use T = θ + 273.','T = 27 + 273.','T = 300 K.'],'300 K','Kelvin temperatures are written without a degree symbol.'),
example('Compressed gas','A gas at 100 kPa occupies 60 cm³. It is compressed at constant temperature to 40 cm³. Find new pressure.',['p₁V₁ = p₂V₂.','p₂ = 100 × 60/40.','p₂ = 150 kPa.'],'150 kPa','Reducing volume increases pressure at constant temperature.')],
questions:[mc('Absolute zero is approximately…',['0 °C','−273 °C','273 °C'],1,'0 K corresponds approximately to −273 °C.'),mc('At constant temperature, halving gas volume makes pressure…',['half','unchanged','double'],2,'p is inversely proportional to V.'),mc('Which expands most for a similar temperature rise?',['Gas','Liquid','Solid'],0,'Gas particle separations change most.'),num('Convert 37 °C to kelvin.',310,'K','37 + 273 = 310 K.'),num('A gas at 80 kPa and 50 cm³ expands isothermally to 100 cm³. Find pressure.',40,'kPa','p₂ = 80 × 50/100 = 40 kPa.'),explain('Explain why gas pressure rises when a sealed rigid container is heated.',['Particles gain average kinetic energy and move faster.','Collisions with the walls become more frequent and more forceful.'])]}
);

lessons.push(
{id:'magnetism-motor',title:'Magnetic fields & the motor effect',topic:'04 · Magnetism',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Map magnetic fields and predict forces on currents and charged particles.',
objectives:['Distinguish permanent and induced magnetism.','Describe fields around currents.','Apply the motor effect.'],
sections:[
section('Magnetic materials','Like poles repel and unlike poles attract. Magnetic materials include iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. Soft iron magnetises and demagnetises readily, making it useful for temporary electromagnets; steel is harder to magnetise but retains magnetism, suiting permanent magnets. Induced magnetism produces attraction to a nearby magnet.','A magnetic material is not necessarily already magnetised.'),
section('Field representation','A magnetic field is a region where a magnetic pole experiences force. Outside a bar magnet, field lines point from north to south. Closer lines represent greater field strength. Plot with a compass for direction or use iron filings to reveal pattern. Field lines never cross.','The compass north-seeking end points along the field direction.'),
section('Fields from currents','A straight current produces circular field lines; reversing current reverses field direction. A solenoid produces a bar-magnet-like field. Increase current, turns per unit length or add a soft-iron core to strengthen an electromagnet.','Switch off current and a soft-iron core loses most of its magnetism.'),
section('Motor effect','A current-carrying conductor in an external magnetic field experiences a force when the current is not parallel to the field. Force increases with field strength, current and conductor length in the field. Reversing current or field reverses force. A split-ring commutator in a simple d.c. motor reverses coil current every half-turn to keep rotation in the same sense.','Use a field–current–force rule consistently; the three directions are mutually perpendicular.')],
formulas:[['Motor-effect force','F = BIL for perpendicular field/current','N'],['Field strength','B = F/(IL)','T']],
examples:[example('Force on a wire','A 0.20 m wire carries 3.0 A perpendicular to a 0.50 T field. Find force.',['Use F = BIL.','F = 0.50 × 3.0 × 0.20.','F = 0.30 N.'],'0.30 N','The perpendicular condition allows direct use of BIL.'),example('Strengthen an electromagnet','A lifting electromagnet is too weak. Give two changes.',['Increase coil current within safe limits.','Increase turns per unit length or add a suitable soft-iron core.','Each change increases the magnetic field.'],'Any two valid changes','Changes must be practical and linked to greater field strength.')],
questions:[mc('Field direction outside a bar magnet is…',['N to S','S to N','towards both poles'],0,'By convention field lines leave N and enter S outside.'),mc('A suitable electromagnet core is often…',['soft iron','glass','copper'],0,'Soft iron magnetises strongly and loses magnetism readily.'),mc('Reversing current in a motor-effect conductor…',['reverses force','removes every field','doubles mass'],0,'Force direction depends on current direction.'),num('B = 0.40 T, I = 2.0 A, L = 0.50 m, perpendicular. Find F.',0.4,'N','F = 0.40 × 2.0 × 0.50 = 0.40 N.'),num('A wire experiences 0.60 N with I = 3.0 A and L = 0.20 m. Find B.',1,'T','B = 0.60/(3.0 × 0.20) = 1.0 T.'),explain('Explain the role of the split-ring commutator in a d.c. motor.',['It reverses current in the coil every half-turn.','This keeps the turning effect in the same rotational sense.'])]},
{id:'induction-transformers',title:'Induction, generators & transformers',topic:'04 · Electromagnetic induction',minutes:35,levels:['Core','Extended'],
intro:'Explain induced e.m.f., alternating generation and efficient power transmission.',
objectives:['Describe electromagnetic induction.','Explain a.c. generation.','Use transformer equations and transmission ideas.'],
sections:[
section('Electromagnetic induction','An e.m.f. is induced when magnetic flux linking a conductor or coil changes. Move a magnet relative to a coil, move a conductor through a field, or change the field. A faster change, stronger field or more coil turns increases induced e.m.f. Reversing the motion reverses induced current.','Relative change matters; a stationary magnet and stationary coil give no continuous induced e.m.f.'),
section('Lenz direction','The induced current creates a magnetic effect opposing the change that produced it. This reflects energy conservation: mechanical work is required to move a magnet against the induced effect. A sensitive meter shows opposite deflections for opposite motion.','The induced field opposes the change, not necessarily the original magnetic field in every situation.'),
section('Generators','A simple a.c. generator rotates a coil in a magnetic field, changing flux linkage and producing alternating e.m.f. Slip rings connect the rotating coil to the external circuit. Increasing rotation speed, field strength, coil area or turns increases output amplitude.','A generator transfers mechanical energy to electrical energy.'),
section('Transformers and the grid','A transformer uses alternating current in a primary coil to produce a changing magnetic field in an iron core and an induced e.m.f. in the secondary. For an ideal transformer, Vp/Vs = Np/Ns and input power equals output power. High-voltage transmission reduces current for the same power, reducing cable loss P = I²R.','Transformers require changing current; steady d.c. does not produce continuous secondary voltage.')],
formulas:[['Transformer turns','Vp/Vs = Np/Ns','no unit'],['Ideal power','VpIp = VsIs','W'],['Cable loss','Ploss = I²R','W']],
examples:[example('Step-up transformer','A transformer has 500 primary turns, 2000 secondary turns and 12 V primary. Find secondary voltage.',['Vs/Vp = Ns/Np.','Vs = 12 × 2000/500.','Vs = 48 V.'],'48 V','More secondary turns gives a step-up transformer.'),example('Transmission loss','A cable resistance is 5.0 Ω. Compare power loss at 20 A and 2.0 A.',['At 20 A: P = 20² × 5.0 = 2000 W.','At 2.0 A: P = 2.0² × 5.0 = 20 W.','Reducing current by factor 10 reduces loss by factor 100.'],'2000 W and 20 W','This is why power is transmitted at high voltage and low current.')],
questions:[mc('An induced e.m.f. requires…',['changing magnetic flux linkage','a stationary unchanging field only','a permanent d.c. current in the secondary'],0,'The linked field must change.'),mc('A transformer operates with…',['alternating current','steady direct current only','no magnetic field'],0,'A changing primary current creates changing flux.'),mc('High transmission voltage reduces losses because it permits…',['larger current','smaller current for the same power','larger cable resistance'],1,'Loss depends on I²R.'),num('Np = 100, Ns = 500, Vp = 6 V. Find Vs.',30,'V','Vs = 6 × 500/100 = 30 V.'),num('A cable carries 4 A and has resistance 3 Ω. Find power loss.',48,'W','P = I²R = 4² × 3 = 48 W.'),explain('Explain why moving a magnet faster through a coil gives a larger induced e.m.f.',['The magnetic flux linkage changes more rapidly.','A greater rate of flux change produces a larger induced e.m.f.'])]},
{id:'atomic-model-fission',title:'Atomic model, fission & fusion',topic:'05 · Nuclear model',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended'],
intro:'Use scattering evidence and compare the two major nuclear energy processes.',
objectives:['Explain alpha-scattering evidence.','Write and balance nuclide changes.','Compare fission with fusion.'],
sections:[
section('From scattering to the nuclear atom','Most alpha particles passed through thin metal foil, showing atoms are mostly empty space. Some deflected through large angles, showing positive charge is concentrated. Very few rebounded, showing a very small, dense nucleus containing most atomic mass.','A rare large deflection requires a concentrated charge and mass, not a diffuse positive sphere.'),
section('Nuclide notation and ions','Nuclide notation places nucleon number A above proton number Z. Neutrons = A − Z. Ions form when atoms gain or lose electrons; the nucleus does not change in ordinary ion formation. Positive ions have lost electrons, negative ions have gained them.','Isotopes differ in neutrons; ions differ in electrons.'),
section('Fission','A heavy nucleus such as uranium can absorb a neutron and split into two lighter nuclei, releasing energy and more neutrons. These neutrons can cause further fissions in a chain reaction. A reactor controls the reaction using control rods and removes thermal energy with coolant.','Conserve nucleon number and proton number in a fission equation.'),
section('Fusion','Fusion joins light nuclei and releases energy because a small mass difference corresponds to released energy. It powers stars but requires extremely high temperature and confinement so nuclei can approach despite electrostatic repulsion. Fusion and fission are both nuclear, but their fuels, conditions and products differ.','Fusion is not ordinary burning and does not occur at everyday temperatures.')],
formulas:[['Neutrons','N = A − Z','no unit'],['Nuclear equation check','sum A and sum Z conserved','—']],
examples:[example('Scattering evidence','What does one alpha particle rebounding suggest?',['A strong repulsive force acted over a very short distance.','Positive charge must be highly concentrated.','The atom contains a tiny dense positive nucleus.'],'Evidence for a small dense positive nucleus','The rarity shows the nucleus occupies a tiny fraction of atomic volume.'),example('Complete a fission balance','A parent total has A = 236 and Z = 92. Products include nuclei with A = 141, Z = 56 and A = 92, Z = 36. How many neutrons are also released?',['Product nuclei account for A = 141 + 92 = 233.','Missing nucleon number = 236 − 233 = 3.','Each neutron has A = 1 and Z = 0, so three neutrons are released.'],'3 neutrons','Charge already balances: 56 + 36 = 92.')],
questions:[mc('Most alpha particles passing straight through show that atoms are mostly…',['empty space','positive material','neutrons only'],0,'There is little matter or charge in most paths.'),mc('A positive ion forms when an atom…',['gains protons','loses electrons','gains neutrons'],1,'Electron loss leaves net positive charge.'),mc('Fusion involves…',['splitting heavy nuclei','joining light nuclei','electron transfer only'],1,'Light nuclei combine and release energy.'),num('A nuclide has A = 56 and Z = 26. Find neutrons.',30,'neutrons','N = 56 − 26 = 30.',0),num('A fission parent has A = 235 and absorbs one neutron. Product nuclei total A = 233. How many neutrons are released?',3,'neutrons','Initial A = 236; missing A = 3.',0),explain('How did alpha scattering show the nucleus is small and positively charged?',['Most alpha particles passed through, so most atomic volume is empty.','Rare large deflections or rebounds require a tiny concentrated positive region.'])]},
{id:'radiation-applications',title:'Radiation uses, hazards & safety',topic:'05 · Applications of radioactivity',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Choose suitable emissions and half-lives, then evaluate risk and protection.',
objectives:['Match radiation properties to applications.','Distinguish exposure from contamination.','Explain safe handling and risk–benefit decisions.'],
sections:[
section('Choosing a source','A source must have suitable penetration, ionisation and half-life. Smoke detectors use a strongly ionising alpha source over a short range. Thickness monitoring may use beta because its partial penetration changes with material thickness. Gamma can sterilise equipment or treat deep tissue because it penetrates strongly.','The best radiation depends on the required range and absorption.'),
section('Medical tracers','A tracer should emit radiation detectable outside the body, commonly gamma, and have a half-life long enough for the test but short enough to reduce dose. Chemical behaviour must target the organ safely. Detectors map the radiation distribution.','An alpha emitter is generally unsuitable for external detection from inside the body.'),
section('Hazards and dose reduction','Ionising radiation can damage cells and DNA, causing mutations or cancer; high doses kill cells. Reduce time, increase distance and use appropriate shielding. Store sources in labelled shielded containers and use handling tools under trained supervision.','Safety reduces risk but does not make ionising radiation harmless.'),
section('Irradiation versus contamination','Irradiation is exposure to radiation from a source and ends when the source is removed. Contamination means radioactive material is present on or inside an object and continues to irradiate until removed or decayed. Internal alpha contamination can be especially damaging.','An irradiated object does not automatically become radioactive or contaminated.')],
formulas:[['Remaining activity','A = A₀(1/2)ⁿ','Bq'],['Number of half-lives','n = t/T½','no unit']],
examples:[example('Choose a tracer half-life','Why is a half-life of minutes or hours often preferred to many years for a medical tracer?',['It lasts long enough to carry out the investigation.','It decays soon afterwards, reducing prolonged dose.','The exact choice depends on the procedure time.'],'Balance detectability with minimum prolonged exposure','Very short half-life could decay before imaging is complete.'),example('Activity after treatment','A source has activity 1600 Bq and half-life 6 h. Find activity after 18 h.',['18/6 = 3 half-lives.','1600 → 800 → 400 → 200 Bq.','Final activity = 200 Bq.'],'200 Bq','Each half-life halves the remaining activity.')],
questions:[mc('Which is commonly suitable for a tracer detected outside the body?',['Gamma','Alpha only','No radiation'],0,'Gamma can penetrate body tissue to a detector.'),mc('Contamination means…',['radioactive material is present on or inside something','radiation briefly passes through only','a detector is switched off'],0,'The material itself remains and emits radiation.'),mc('Increasing distance from a source generally…',['reduces exposure','increases activity','lengthens its half-life'],0,'Radiation intensity usually falls with distance.'),num('Activity is 960 Bq. Find it after two half-lives.',240,'Bq','960 → 480 → 240 Bq.'),num('A half-life is 4 days. How many half-lives pass in 20 days?',5,'','20/4 = 5.',0),explain('Why is beta more suitable than alpha for monitoring thin-sheet thickness?',['Beta partially penetrates the sheet, so detector count changes with thickness.','Alpha would be stopped too easily and could not provide a useful transmitted signal.'])]},
{id:'stellar-cosmology',title:'Solar System, stars & cosmology',topic:'06 · Space physics detail',minutes:35,levels:['Core','Extended'],
intro:'Connect orbital patterns to stellar evolution and the evidence for cosmic expansion.',
objectives:['Describe Solar System structure and orbital trends.','Outline stellar evolution by mass.','Use redshift and Hubble’s law as evidence.'],
sections:[
section('Solar System patterns','Planets orbit the Sun, moons orbit planets, and smaller bodies include asteroids and comets. Gravity supplies the inward force. For planets, larger orbital radius generally means longer orbital period and lower orbital speed. Geostationary satellites orbit above the equator in the direction of Earth rotation with a 24-hour period, staying above one point.','Geostationary orbit has specific plane, direction and period requirements.'),
section('Birth and main sequence','Stars form when gravity pulls gas and dust in a nebula together. Contraction raises temperature until hydrogen fusion begins. During the main sequence, inward gravitational effects balance outward pressure effects. The star’s initial mass strongly affects its lifetime and final stages.','A more massive star uses fuel faster despite having more fuel.'),
section('Stellar evolution','A Sun-like star becomes a red giant, sheds outer layers as a planetary nebula and leaves a white dwarf. A massive star becomes a red supergiant, undergoes a supernova and leaves a neutron star or, for the most massive remnants, a black hole. Supernovae distribute heavy elements into space.','The two paths diverge mainly because of initial mass.'),
section('Expansion evidence','Redshift means observed spectral wavelengths are longer than laboratory values, indicating recession. Hubble’s law v = H₀d describes a large-scale proportionality between recession speed and distance. This supports an expanding Universe. Cosmic microwave background radiation supports a hot, dense early state. An estimate of 1/H₀ gives the expansion timescale, with unit conversion.','Redshift is a spectral measurement, not simply a galaxy looking red.')],
formulas:[['Orbital speed','v = 2πr/T','m/s'],['Hubble law','v = H₀d','km/s when matching units'],['Expansion timescale','t ≈ 1/H₀','s after conversion']],
examples:[example('Hubble-law speed','Use H₀ = 70 km/s per Mpc. Find recession speed at 50 Mpc.',['v = H₀d.','v = 70 × 50.','v = 3500 km/s.'],'3500 km/s','Use compatible Mpc units with the quoted Hubble constant.'),example('Geostationary requirement','Why must a geostationary satellite have a 24 h period above the equator?',['Its angular speed must equal Earth rotation.','Its orbit must be in the equatorial plane and same direction.','Then it remains above the same surface longitude.'],'It matches Earth’s rotation','A tilted or opposite orbit would move across the sky.')],
questions:[mc('A Sun-like star ends as a…',['white dwarf','neutron star in every case','new planet'],0,'It sheds outer layers and leaves a white dwarf.'),mc('A massive-star supernova may leave a…',['neutron star or black hole','main-sequence Sun only','comet'],0,'The remnant depends on mass.'),mc('Hubble’s law relates recession speed to…',['galaxy distance','planet mass only','star temperature only'],0,'More distant galaxies generally recede faster.'),num('H₀ = 70 km/s/Mpc and d = 20 Mpc. Find v.',1400,'km/s','v = 70 × 20 = 1400 km/s.'),num('A satellite completes a circular path of 84 000 km in 12 h. Find average orbital speed in km/h.',7000,'km/h','v = 84 000/12 = 7000 km/h.'),explain('Give two observations supporting a hot, expanding Universe.',['Systematic redshift and the distance–recession-speed relationship support expansion.','Cosmic microwave background radiation supports a hot, dense early Universe.'])]},
{id:'practical-skills',title:'Experimental planning & data analysis',topic:'07 · Paper 5/6 skills',minutes:40,levels:['Practical'],
intro:'Plan fair tests, process evidence and write improvements that answer the question asked.',
objectives:['Identify variables and design a repeatable method.','Build tables and graphs correctly.','Evaluate uncertainty and propose specific improvements.'],
sections:[
section('Plan a fair test','State the independent variable you change, dependent variable you measure, and important controls. Describe apparatus, a safe repeatable method and a suitable range with at least five values where practical. Explain how each control is maintained. A diagram can communicate arrangement clearly.','“Keep everything the same” is not enough; name the relevant control variables.'),
section('Tables and measurements','Put the independent variable in the first column and include units in headings, not every cell. Record consistent decimal places justified by the instrument. Repeat readings and calculate a mean when appropriate. Do not average an identified anomaly without investigation.','Raw readings and calculated values should be distinguishable.'),
section('Graphs and gradients','Choose axes so data use at least half the grid, use simple scales and label quantity/unit. Plot small clear crosses and draw a best-fit line or smooth curve rather than joining dot to dot. For a gradient, choose two widely separated points on the best-fit line, not necessarily measured points. Show the triangle and units.','A larger gradient triangle reduces fractional reading uncertainty.'),
section('Evaluate evidence','Random uncertainty causes scatter; repeats and averaging help. Systematic error shifts results consistently and needs calibration or method change. Improvements must identify a problem and explain how the change reduces it: use light gates to reduce reaction-time effect, insulation to reduce heat loss, or a set square to avoid parallax.','“Use better equipment” earns little unless you specify what and why.')],
formulas:[['Mean','Σ readings / number','same unit'],['Gradient','Δy/Δx','y-unit/x-unit'],['Percentage difference','|a−b| / reference × 100%','%']],
examples:[example('Gradient from a best-fit line','Two well-separated points on a best-fit line are (2.0 s, 5.0 m) and (8.0 s, 23.0 m). Find gradient.',['Δy = 23.0 − 5.0 = 18.0 m.','Δx = 8.0 − 2.0 = 6.0 s.','Gradient = 18.0/6.0 = 3.0 m/s.'],'3.0 m/s','Use points on the line and include gradient units.'),example('Write an improvement','A student times a trolley over 0.50 m using a handheld stopwatch. Improve the timing.',['Human reaction time is significant compared with the short travel time.','Use two light gates connected to an electronic timer.','This starts and stops timing at defined positions and removes most reaction-time delay.'],'Use light gates and explain the error reduced','Simply saying “repeat” does not remove reaction-time bias.')],
questions:[mc('The independent variable is the quantity that is…',['deliberately changed','measured as the outcome','always ignored'],0,'It is selected and varied by the experimenter.'),mc('A graph should usually use…',['a tiny corner of the grid','at least about half the available grid','no units'],1,'A large scale improves reading precision.'),mc('A systematic error is best addressed by…',['calibration or method correction','averaging identical biased readings only','removing units'],0,'The consistent shift must be identified and corrected.'),num('Readings are 4.2, 4.4 and 4.3 s. Find mean.',4.3,'s','Mean = (4.2 + 4.4 + 4.3)/3 = 4.3 s.'),num('A graph rises 15 N over a horizontal change of 0.30 m. Find gradient.',50,'N/m','Gradient = 15/0.30 = 50 N/m.'),explain('Write the key elements of a strong experimental plan.',['Identify independent, dependent and control variables, apparatus, range, repeats and a repeatable safe method.','State how measurements are taken and how data will be processed or graphed.'])]}
);

lessons.push(
{id:'light-refraction',title:'Light, refraction & total internal reflection',topic:'03 · Light',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Draw ray diagrams, use refractive index and explain optical fibres.',
objectives:['Apply the laws of reflection and refraction.','Calculate refractive index and critical angle.','Explain total internal reflection.'],
sections:[
section('Reflection','The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, measured from the normal. A plane mirror forms a virtual, upright, laterally inverted image of the same size, as far behind the mirror as the object is in front. Construct images by extending reflected rays backwards.','Angles are measured from the normal, not from the mirror surface.'),
section('Refraction','Light changes speed at a boundary. From air into glass, it slows and bends towards the normal; from glass into air, it speeds up and bends away. Frequency remains constant while wavelength changes. Along the normal, speed changes without a direction change.','Refraction is caused by a speed change, not by the light being pulled towards the normal.'),
section('Refractive index','For light from air into a transparent material, n = sin i / sin r and n = c/v. A larger n means lower light speed in the material. Use angles from the normal and ensure the calculator is in degrees.','The incident angle belongs in the numerator for n = sin i/sin r.'),
section('Critical angle and TIR','Total internal reflection occurs only when light travels from higher refractive index to lower refractive index and the incidence angle is greater than the critical angle. At the critical angle, the refracted ray travels along the boundary. Optical fibres use repeated TIR for communication and medical imaging.','TIR cannot occur for light entering glass from air.')],
formulas:[['Refractive index','n = sin i / sin r','no unit'],['Speed form','n = c/v','no unit'],['Critical angle','sin c = 1/n, material to air','degrees']],
examples:[example('Refractive index','A ray from air enters glass with i = 45° and r = 28°. Find n.',['n = sin45°/sin28°.','n ≈ 0.7071/0.4695.','n ≈ 1.51.'],'1.51','Use angles measured from the normal.'),example('Critical angle','A transparent material has n = 1.50. Find critical angle to air.',['sin c = 1/n = 1/1.50.','c = sin⁻¹(0.6667).','c ≈ 41.8°.'],'41.8°','TIR occurs for internal incidence angles greater than this value.')],
questions:[mc('A plane-mirror image is…',['real and inverted','virtual and upright','smaller and real'],1,'Reflected rays only appear to come from behind the mirror.'),mc('Light entering glass from air usually bends…',['towards the normal','away from the normal','without changing speed'],0,'It slows in the higher-index material.'),mc('TIR requires light to travel…',['from lower to higher index','from higher to lower index','only through air'],1,'It also requires incidence angle greater than critical angle.'),num('Light speed in a material is 2.0e8 m/s. Use c = 3.0e8 m/s. Find n.',1.5,'','n = c/v = 3.0e8/2.0e8 = 1.5.',0.01),num('For n = 2.0, find sin of the critical angle.',0.5,'','sin c = 1/n = 0.5.',0.001),explain('State the two conditions needed for total internal reflection.',['Light travels from a higher refractive index medium towards a lower-index medium.','The incidence angle is greater than the critical angle.'])]},
{id:'lenses',title:'Thin lenses & image formation',topic:'03 · Optics',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Construct lens images and connect focal length to magnification.',
objectives:['Distinguish converging and diverging lenses.','Construct principal-ray diagrams.','Calculate magnification.'],
sections:[
section('Converging and diverging lenses','A converging lens is thicker in the centre and brings parallel rays to a principal focus. A diverging lens is thinner in the centre and spreads parallel rays as if they came from a focus. Focal length is the distance from optical centre to principal focus.','A focus for a diverging lens is virtual for parallel incident rays.'),
section('Principal rays','For a converging lens: a ray parallel to the axis refracts through the far focus; a ray through the optical centre continues approximately straight; a ray through the near focus emerges parallel. The intersection of refracted rays locates a real image. Backward extensions locate a virtual image.','Use at least two accurately drawn rays from the same object point.'),
section('Image properties','With an object beyond 2F, a converging lens forms a smaller real inverted image between F and 2F. At 2F, image is same size at 2F. Between F and 2F, image is larger, real and inverted beyond 2F. Inside F, image is larger, virtual and upright.','Real images can be projected onto a screen; virtual images cannot.'),
section('Magnification and uses','Linear magnification = image height/object height. Cameras form real images; magnifying glasses use a converging lens with the object inside F to form a larger virtual image. Corrective lenses alter the convergence of light entering the eye.','Use the same length unit for image and object heights.')],
formulas:[['Linear magnification','m = image height / object height','no unit'],['Also for a thin-lens ray diagram','m = image distance / object distance','no unit']],
examples:[example('Image magnification','An object 2.0 cm high forms an image 6.0 cm high. Find magnification.',['m = image height/object height.','m = 6.0/2.0.','m = 3.0.'],'3.0','Magnification has no unit when both heights use the same unit.'),example('Find image height','A lens produces magnification 0.40 from an object 5.0 cm high. Find image height.',['image height = m × object height.','image height = 0.40 × 5.0.','image height = 2.0 cm.'],'2.0 cm','A magnification below one means the image is smaller.')],
questions:[mc('A real lens image can be…',['projected on a screen','seen only in a plane mirror','formed without refracted rays'],0,'Real rays meet at a real image.'),mc('A ray through the optical centre of a thin lens…',['reverses direction','continues approximately straight','must pass through the focus first'],1,'This is a principal construction ray.'),mc('A magnifying glass places the object…',['inside the focal length','at infinity only','beyond 2F only'],0,'It produces a larger upright virtual image.'),num('Image height is 9 cm and object height is 3 cm. Find magnification.',3,'','m = 9/3 = 3.',0),num('Magnification is 2.5 and object height is 4 cm. Find image height.',10,'cm','image height = 2.5 × 4 = 10 cm.'),explain('Distinguish a real image from a virtual image.',['A real image forms where rays actually converge and can be projected on a screen.','A virtual image forms where rays only appear to originate and cannot be projected directly.'])]},
{id:'em-spectrum',title:'Electromagnetic spectrum & communication',topic:'03 · Electromagnetic waves',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended'],
intro:'Order the spectrum, connect regions to uses and compare analogue with digital signals.',
objectives:['Recall the electromagnetic spectrum order.','Link uses and hazards to wave properties.','Explain communication choices and digital regeneration.'],
sections:[
section('One family of waves','All electromagnetic waves are transverse and travel at approximately 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum. In increasing frequency: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma. Higher frequency means shorter wavelength in vacuum and generally greater photon energy.','Remember the spectrum as one continuous family, not unrelated waves.'),
section('Uses','Radio carries broadcasting and some communications. Microwaves serve satellites, mobile phones and heating. Infrared is used in thermal imaging, heaters and remote controls. Visible light and infrared carry data in optical fibres. Ultraviolet can cause fluorescence and sterilise; X-rays image dense structures; gamma radiation can treat cancer and sterilise equipment.','A useful property can also create a hazard at excessive exposure.'),
section('Hazards','Microwaves can heat body tissue; infrared can burn skin; ultraviolet damages skin and eyes and raises cancer risk; X-rays and gamma are ionising and can damage cells or cause mutations. Reduce exposure using shielding, distance and time controls appropriate to the radiation.','Ionising risk is associated with ultraviolet at the high-frequency end, X-rays and gamma in this syllabus context.'),
section('Communication signals','Analogue signals vary continuously; digital signals use discrete states, usually 0 and 1. Digital signals can be regenerated accurately, extending range and supporting high data rates. Microwaves penetrate some walls and need short aerials; optical fibres support high data rates because glass transmits suitable visible and infrared wavelengths.','Digital does not mean immune to noise; regeneration reduces accumulated distortion.')],
formulas:[['Wave relationship','c = fλ in vacuum','m/s'],['Frequency','f = c/λ','Hz']],
examples:[example('Radio wavelength','A radio wave has frequency 100 MHz. Find wavelength in vacuum.',['100 MHz = 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz.','λ = c/f = 3.0 × 10⁸/1.0 × 10⁸.','λ = 3.0 m.'],'3.0 m','Convert MHz to Hz before calculation.'),example('Identify a region','A wave has wavelength 600 nm. Identify its broad region.',['600 nm = 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ m.','This lies in the approximate visible range.','It would be seen as orange-red light.'],'Visible light','Exact boundaries are approximate; broad region identification is sufficient.')],
questions:[mc('Which order has increasing frequency?',['gamma, X-ray, radio','radio, infrared, visible','visible, infrared, microwave'],1,'Radio has lower frequency than infrared and visible.'),mc('Which is ionising?',['Radio','Infrared','X-ray'],2,'X-rays can ionise atoms and damage cells.'),mc('A main digital-signalling benefit is…',['accurate regeneration','infinite speed','no need for a receiver'],0,'Regeneration restores discrete signal levels.'),num('A wave has f = 6.0e14 Hz. Use c = 3.0e8 m/s. Find wavelength in metres.',5e-7,'m','λ = 3.0e8/6.0e14 = 5.0e−7 m.',1e-9),num('A microwave has wavelength 0.030 m. Find frequency using c = 3.0e8 m/s.',1e10,'Hz','f = 3.0e8/0.030 = 1.0e10 Hz.',1e8),explain('Why are optical fibres suitable for high-speed data communication?',['Glass is transparent to suitable visible or infrared wavelengths and guides light by total internal reflection.','Short wavelengths/high frequencies can carry high data rates with low loss over suitable links.'])]},
{id:'electrostatics',title:'Electrostatics & electric fields',topic:'04 · Electric charge',minutes:25,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Explain charging by electron transfer and represent electric fields.',
objectives:['Describe attraction and repulsion.','Explain charging by friction.','Draw and interpret electric fields.'],
sections:[
section('Two types of charge','Positive repels positive, negative repels negative, and unlike charges attract. A charged object can attract a neutral insulator because charges within it shift slightly, producing a closer opposite effect. Repulsion is the reliable test that two objects carry like charge.','Attraction alone does not prove two objects have opposite net charges.'),
section('Charging by friction','In solids, charging by friction transfers electrons. An object gaining electrons becomes negative; losing electrons makes it positive. Protons remain bound in nuclei. Charge is conserved, so equal and opposite net charges can appear on the two rubbed materials.','Only negative charge is transferred in this simple electron model.'),
section('Conductors and insulators','Conductors have mobile charge carriers, so excess charge spreads or flows to earth. In insulators, charge is less free to move and can remain localised. Test materials in a simple circuit or use electrostatic behaviour, controlling humidity and avoiding unintended earthing.','Holding a conductor by hand may discharge it through your body to earth.'),
section('Electric fields','An electric field is a region where a charge experiences force. Direction is the force direction on a positive test charge. Lines point away from positive charges and towards negative charges; closer spacing represents greater field strength. Uniform parallel lines occur between oppositely charged parallel plates away from edges.','Field lines never cross because the field has one direction at each point.')],
formulas:[['Charge flow','Q = It','C'],['Electric field direction','force direction on positive test charge','—']],
examples:[example('Electron transfer','A plastic rod gains electrons when rubbed. What charge does it acquire?',['Electrons carry negative charge.','Gaining electrons gives an excess of negative charge.','The rod becomes negatively charged.'],'Negative','The rubbing material loses the same amount of negative charge in an isolated pair.'),example('Charge from current','A current of 0.20 A flows for 30 s. Find charge transferred.',['Use Q = It.','Q = 0.20 × 30.','Q = 6.0 C.'],'6.0 C','One ampere is one coulomb per second.')],
questions:[mc('Like charges…',['attract','repel','have no effect'],1,'Charges of the same sign repel.'),mc('Charging a solid by friction transfers…',['protons','electrons','neutrons'],1,'Electrons can transfer between materials.'),mc('Electric field direction is defined using…',['a positive test charge','a neutron','a negative test mass'],0,'It is the direction of force on a positive charge.'),num('A current of 0.50 A flows for 8 s. Find charge.',4,'C','Q = 0.50 × 8 = 4 C.'),num('12 C passes in 3 s. Find current.',4,'A','I = Q/t = 12/3 = 4 A.'),explain('Why can a charged rod attract a neutral object?',['Charges in the neutral object redistribute or polarise.','The induced opposite charge is closer, giving a net attraction.'])]},
{id:'circuit-components',title:'Circuit components & potential dividers',topic:'04 · Circuit behaviour',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended','Practical'],
intro:'Interpret current–voltage behaviour and design sensing circuits.',
objectives:['Describe I–V characteristics.','Explain thermistors and LDRs.','Calculate a potential divider output.'],
sections:[
section('I–V characteristics','An ohmic resistor at constant temperature has a straight I–V graph through the origin. A filament lamp becomes hotter as current rises, so resistance increases and the graph curves. A diode conducts strongly in one direction after a threshold but has very small reverse current in normal operation.','Resistance at a point is V/I; graph gradient depends on which quantity is on each axis.'),
section('Sensors','An LDR has lower resistance in brighter light. A common NTC thermistor has lower resistance at higher temperature. These components convert environmental changes into resistance changes and can be combined with a fixed resistor to produce a changing voltage.','State the direction of resistance change before predicting circuit output.'),
section('Potential divider','Two series resistors share supply voltage. The output across one resistor is Vin × that resistor/(sum of series resistances). Replacing one resistor with an LDR or thermistor makes a sensing circuit. Output direction depends on where the output is measured.','Draw or inspect which component the output is across.'),
section('Practical measurements','Measure current with an ammeter in series and potential difference with a voltmeter in parallel. Vary the supply or variable resistor, record paired values and plot a suitable graph. Limit current to protect components and allow a filament to reach a steady reading.','Connect meters correctly before switching on.')],
formulas:[['Resistance','R = V/I','Ω'],['Potential divider','Vout = Vin × R₂/(R₁ + R₂)','V']],
examples:[example('Divider output','A 12 V supply is across 2 kΩ and 4 kΩ in series. Output is across 4 kΩ. Find Vout.',['Total resistance = 6 kΩ.','Fraction across 4 kΩ = 4/6.','Vout = 12 × 4/6 = 8.0 V.'],'8.0 V','Series voltage share is proportional to resistance.'),example('Lamp resistance','A lamp operates at 6.0 V and 0.50 A. Find operating resistance.',['R = V/I.','R = 6.0/0.50.','R = 12 Ω.'],'12 Ω','The lamp resistance may differ at another current because temperature changes.')],
questions:[mc('An LDR resistance decreases when light becomes…',['brighter','dimmer','unchanged'],0,'More light lowers LDR resistance.'),mc('An NTC thermistor resistance decreases when temperature…',['falls','rises','stays fixed'],1,'NTC means negative temperature coefficient.'),mc('A diode mainly allows current…',['equally both ways','in one direction','only at zero voltage'],1,'It has strongly directional conduction.'),num('A resistor has 10 V across it and current 0.25 A. Find R.',40,'Ω','R = 10/0.25 = 40 Ω.'),num('Vin = 9 V, R₁ = 2 kΩ, R₂ = 1 kΩ; output is across R₂. Find Vout.',3,'V','Vout = 9 × 1/(2+1) = 3 V.'),explain('Explain why a filament-lamp I–V graph curves.',['Increasing current heats the filament.','Higher temperature increases its resistance, so current is no longer proportional to voltage.'])]},
{id:'logic-safety',title:'Logic gates & electrical safety',topic:'04 · Digital and mains electricity',minutes:30,levels:['Core','Extended'],
intro:'Use truth tables and explain how mains safety devices protect users.',
objectives:['Interpret AND, OR and NOT gates.','Construct simple logic outputs.','Explain fuses, earthing and insulation.'],
sections:[
section('Digital states and gates','Digital signals have discrete states represented as 0 and 1. AND outputs 1 only when both inputs are 1. OR outputs 1 when at least one input is 1. NOT inverts its single input. Build a truth table by listing every input combination systematically.','For two binary inputs there are four possible combinations.'),
section('Logic systems','Sensors can feed logic circuits through suitable interfaces. Translate words carefully: “both” suggests AND, “either or both” suggests OR, and “not” suggests inversion. More complex systems combine gates; work from inputs towards the output one gate at a time.','Do not confuse inclusive OR with “exactly one”.'),
section('Mains hazards','Damaged insulation, wet conditions and contact with live conductors can allow dangerous current through the body. Excessive current heats wires and can cause fire. Switches and fuses belong in the live conductor so disconnection isolates the appliance from the high-potential supply.','A neutral wire is not treated as a safe touchable conductor.'),
section('Protection','A fuse melts when current exceeds its rating; a circuit breaker opens rapidly and can be reset. An earth wire connects a metal case to earth, creating a low-resistance fault path so a large current disconnects the supply. Double-insulated appliances use insulating cases and need no earth wire.','Choose a fuse rating just above the normal operating current.')],
formulas:[['Operating current','I = P/V','A'],['Two-input combinations','2² = 4','rows']],
examples:[example('Truth-table output','An alarm output is A AND NOT B. Find output when A = 1 and B = 0.',['NOT B = 1.','A AND NOT B = 1 AND 1.','Output = 1.'],'1','Evaluate NOT before the final AND.'),example('Choose a fuse','A 920 W appliance uses 230 V. Find operating current and choose between 3 A and 5 A fuses.',['I = P/V = 920/230 = 4.0 A.','A 3 A fuse would melt in normal use.','Choose 5 A, the available rating just above 4.0 A.'],'4.0 A; choose 5 A','A much larger fuse would give poorer overcurrent protection.')],
questions:[mc('AND outputs 1 when…',['both inputs are 1','either input is 0','both inputs are 0'],0,'That is the AND truth condition.'),mc('A fuse is connected in the…',['earth wire','live wire','appliance case'],1,'It must disconnect the live supply.'),mc('Double insulation usually means…',['two batteries','an insulating case with no exposed conductive case requiring earth','two earth wires'],1,'The user is separated from live parts by insulating barriers.'),num('A 1150 W appliance operates at 230 V. Find current.',5,'A','I = 1150/230 = 5 A.'),num('How many input combinations are in a three-input truth table?',8,'','2³ = 8 combinations.',0),explain('Explain how earthing and a fuse protect against a live wire touching a metal case.',['The earth wire provides a low-resistance path, keeping the case near earth potential.','The large fault current melts the fuse or trips protection, disconnecting the live supply.'])]}
);
